from cloudkit import *
CA = 'l43_aws.txt'; C = 'l43.txt'; CD = 'l43_data.txt'
def seca(a, b=None): return capsec(CA, a, b)
PRAC = practical('Part A builds network, storage and data virtualization on your own machine; Part B does the same ideas in AWS. Every step shows a <b>UI way</b> and a <b>CLI way</b>. Linux CLI steps need root (WSL2 or any Linux VM is fine). <b>Part B outputs come from the moto AWS emulator</b> — IDs differ on a real account; everything in Part B is free except a few cents for the EBS volume and snapshot if left running.', [
    ('A1 · Two isolated networks joined by a virtual cable', uicli(
        ['VirtualBox: create (or clone) two small VMs, <code>kitchen</code> and <code>billing</code>.', 'Each VM → [[Settings]] → [[Network]] → Adapter 1 → Attached to [[Internal Network]] → Name <code>kitchen-net</code> → [[OK]].', 'In each VM set an IP: kitchen <code>10.10.0.1/24</code>, billing <code>10.10.0.2/24</code> (Ubuntu: [[Settings]] → [[Network]] → [[IPv4]] → Manual).', 'From kitchen: open a terminal and <code>ping 10.10.0.2</code>.'],
        code('bash', 'terminal (root)', 'ip netns add kitchen; ip netns add billing\nip link add veth-k type veth peer name veth-b\nip link set veth-k netns kitchen; ip link set veth-b netns billing\nip -n kitchen addr add 10.10.0.1/24 dev veth-k; ip -n kitchen link set veth-k up\nip -n billing addr add 10.10.0.2/24 dev veth-b; ip -n billing link set veth-b up\nip netns exec kitchen ping -c 2 10.10.0.2', capsec(C, '== 1)', '== 2)')), ui_label='UI way — VirtualBox internal network'),
        'VirtualBox’s “Internal Network” is a virtual switch inside the hypervisor; netns + veth is the same idea without VMs. Both give 0% loss with no physical cable.'),
    ('A2 · Add a firewall rule inside one network', uicli(
        ['In the <code>billing</code> VM (Ubuntu desktop): install <b>gufw</b> (Firewall Configuration) → [[Status: On]] → [[Rules]] → [[+]] → Policy [[Deny]], Direction [[In]], Protocol ICMP from 10.10.0.1.', 'On a Windows VM: [[Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security]] → [[Inbound Rules]] → [[New Rule…]].', 'Ping again from kitchen: now it fails.'],
        code('bash', 'terminal (root)', 'ip netns exec billing iptables -A INPUT -p icmp -j DROP\nip netns exec kitchen ping -c 2 -W 1 10.10.0.2     # 100% loss now\nip netns del kitchen; ip netns del billing')),
        'A software firewall on a software network — exactly what a security group is in a VPC (B4).'),
    ('A3 · Pool two disks into one (storage virtualization)', uicli(
        ['Windows 10/11: attach two empty USB/virtual disks.', '[[Settings]] → [[System]] → [[Storage]] → [[Advanced storage settings]] → [[Storage Spaces]] → [[Create a storage pool]] → select both disks → [[Create]].', 'Create a Storage Space (virtual disk) from the pool with any size and resiliency → format it.'],
        code('bash', 'terminal (root, Linux LVM)', 'truncate -s 200M d1.img d2.img\nL1=$(losetup -f --show d1.img); L2=$(losetup -f --show d2.img)\npvcreate $L1 $L2; vgcreate pool $L1 $L2\nvgs -o vg_name,pv_count,vg_size\nlvcreate -L 300M -n data pool        # a volume bigger than either disk', capsec(C, '== 3)', '== 4)')), ui_label='UI way — Windows Storage Spaces'),
        'Storage Spaces (Windows) and LVM (Linux) do the same job: many disks → one pool → virtual disks of any size. (Our lab container could not run lvcreate; on a normal Linux machine it works.)'),
    ('A4 · Thin disks and linked clones', uicli(
        ['VirtualBox: [[File]] → [[Tools]] → [[Virtual Media Manager]] → [[Create]] → VDI, [[Dynamically allocated]], 10 GB → note the tiny actual size.', 'Select a VM → [[Clone]] → [[Linked clone]] → [[Finish]]: the new VM’s disk starts almost empty and reads the rest from the original snapshot.'],
        code('bash', 'terminal', 'qemu-img create -f qcow2 base.qcow2 10G\nqemu-img create -f qcow2 -b base.qcow2 -F qcow2 clone1.qcow2\nqemu-img info clone1.qcow2 | grep -E "virtual size|disk size|backing"', capsec(C, '== 4)')), ui_label='UI way — VirtualBox'),
        'Thin provisioning + copy-on-write: 10 GiB promised, 196 KiB used.'),
    ('A5 · One query over three sources (data virtualization)', uicli(
        ['Install DuckDB and run <code>duckdb -ui</code> — it opens DuckDB’s local web UI in your browser.', 'In a notebook cell run <code>INSTALL sqlite; LOAD sqlite; ATTACH \'crm.db\' AS crm (TYPE sqlite);</code>', 'Paste the SELECT on the right → [[Run]] and see one result table.', 'Cloud UI equivalent: [[Athena]] console → [[Query editor]] → choose a federated data source → run SQL.'],
        code('sql', 'duckdb', "INSTALL sqlite; LOAD sqlite; ATTACH 'crm.db' AS crm (TYPE sqlite);\nSELECT c.zone, o.city, count(*) AS orders, sum(o.amount) AS revenue,\n       round(avg(r.stars),1) AS avg_stars\nFROM 'orders.csv' o JOIN 'cities.parquet' c USING (city)\nJOIN crm.ratings r USING (order_id)\nGROUP BY ALL ORDER BY revenue DESC;", capsec(CD, '== 5)')), ui_label='UI way — DuckDB UI / Athena'),
        'The sample files are made by <code>l43_data.py</code> (in tools/authoring/src/cloud-fundamentals/lab/jobs/m4).'),
    ('B1 · Create a VPC (virtual network)', uicli(
        ['AWS Console → [[VPC]] → [[Your VPCs]] → [[Create VPC]].', 'Resources to create: [[VPC only]]; Name tag <code>tiffingo-vpc</code>; IPv4 CIDR <code>10.10.0.0/16</code>.', 'Click [[Create VPC]] and note the VPC ID.'],
        code('bash', 'terminal', "aws ec2 create-vpc --cidr-block 10.10.0.0/16 \\\n  --tag-specifications 'ResourceType=vpc,Tags=[{Key=Name,Value=tiffingo-vpc}]' \\\n  --query Vpc.VpcId --output text", seca('== 1)', '== 2)')), ui_label='UI way — AWS Management Console'),
        'A private network of 65,536 addresses exists in a second — an overlay (type 2), nothing physical installed. “VPC and more” in the console would also create subnets and route tables for you.'),
    ('B2 · Two subnets in two Availability Zones', uicli(
        ['[[VPC]] → [[Subnets]] → [[Create subnet]] → VPC ID: <code>tiffingo-vpc</code>.', 'Subnet 1: name <code>kitchen</code>, AZ [[ap-south-1a]], CIDR <code>10.10.1.0/24</code>.', '[[Add new subnet]] → <code>billing</code>, AZ [[ap-south-1b]], CIDR <code>10.10.2.0/24</code> → [[Create subnet]].', 'Look at the [[Available IPv4 addresses]] column: 251.'],
        code('bash', 'terminal', "aws ec2 create-subnet --vpc-id $VPC --cidr-block 10.10.1.0/24 --availability-zone ap-south-1a\naws ec2 create-subnet --vpc-id $VPC --cidr-block 10.10.2.0/24 --availability-zone ap-south-1b\naws ec2 describe-subnets --filters Name=vpc-id,Values=$VPC \\\n  --query 'Subnets[].[CidrBlock,AvailabilityZone,AvailableIpAddressCount]' --output table", seca('== 2)', '== 3)')), ui_label='UI way — AWS Management Console'),
        '251, not 256: AWS reserves 5 addresses in every subnet.'),
    ('B3 · Look at the virtual router', uicli(
        ['[[VPC]] → [[Route tables]] → select the table whose VPC is <code>tiffingo-vpc</code> (Main = Yes).', 'Open the [[Routes]] tab: one route <code>10.10.0.0/16 → local</code>.'],
        code('bash', 'terminal', "aws ec2 describe-route-tables --filters Name=vpc-id,Values=$VPC \\\n  --query 'RouteTables[].Routes[].[DestinationCidrBlock,GatewayId,State]' --output table", seca('== 3)', '== 4)')), ui_label='UI way — AWS Management Console'),
        'The “local” route is step 2 of the packet animation: anything inside the VPC is delivered by the overlay.'),
    ('B4 · A virtual firewall (security group)', uicli(
        ['[[VPC]] (or [[EC2]]) → [[Security groups]] → [[Create security group]].', 'Name <code>kitchen-sg</code>, Description “kitchen API”, VPC <code>tiffingo-vpc</code>.', '[[Inbound rules]] → [[Add rule]] → Type [[HTTPS]] (443) → Source [[Custom]] <code>10.10.2.0/24</code> → [[Create security group]].'],
        code('bash', 'terminal', 'SG=$(aws ec2 create-security-group --group-name kitchen-sg --description "kitchen API" \\\n  --vpc-id $VPC --query GroupId --output text)\naws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress --group-id $SG --protocol tcp --port 443 --cidr 10.10.2.0/24', seca('== 4)', '== 5)')), ui_label='UI way — AWS Management Console'),
        'Only billing’s subnet may call the kitchen API. The rule is enforced on the Nitro card, outside the VM.'),
    ('B5 · A virtual disk (EBS volume)', uicli(
        ['[[EC2]] → [[Elastic Block Store]] → [[Volumes]] → [[Create volume]].', 'Volume type [[gp3]], Size <code>20</code> GiB, Availability Zone [[ap-south-1a]] → [[Create volume]].', 'State goes [[Creating]] → [[Available]].'],
        code('bash', 'terminal', 'aws ec2 create-volume --availability-zone ap-south-1a --size 20 --volume-type gp3', seca('== 5)', '== 6)')), ui_label='UI way — AWS Management Console'),
        'Block storage (type 5) carved from the AZ’s storage fleet — usable only in ap-south-1a.'),
    ('B6 · Move it to another AZ with a snapshot', uicli(
        ['Select the volume → [[Actions]] → [[Create snapshot]] → Description “before menu upgrade” → [[Create snapshot]].', '[[Snapshots]] → wait for [[Completed]] → select it → [[Actions]] → [[Create volume from snapshot]] → AZ [[ap-south-1b]], type gp3 → [[Create volume]].'],
        code('bash', 'terminal', 'SN=$(aws ec2 create-snapshot --volume-id $V --description "before menu upgrade" \\\n  --query SnapshotId --output text)\naws ec2 wait snapshot-completed --snapshot-ids $SN     # real account: wait\naws ec2 create-volume --availability-zone ap-south-1b --snapshot-id $SN --volume-type gp3', seca('== 6)', '== 7)')), ui_label='UI way — AWS Management Console'),
        'Snapshots live in S3 (Regional), so they can be restored in any AZ — the only way to “move” an EBS volume.')],
    cleanup='Console: delete both volumes ([[Volumes]] → [[Actions]] → [[Delete volume]]), the snapshot, the security group, both subnets and the VPC ([[Your VPCs]] → [[Actions]] → [[Delete VPC]] removes subnets/route tables too). CLI: <code>delete-volume</code> ×2, <code>delete-snapshot</code>, <code>delete-security-group</code>, <code>delete-subnet</code> ×2, <code>delete-vpc</code>. Unattached volumes and snapshots are billed monthly until deleted. Locally: <code>ip netns del</code>, <code>vgremove pool</code>, <code>losetup -d</code>, delete .img/.qcow2 files; VirtualBox: remove test VMs.'.replace('[[', '<b>').replace(']]', '</b>'))
